# Local-only Worker secrets for `wrangler dev`. Copy this file to `.dev.vars`
# (gitignored) and fill in real values from the Clerk/Resend dashboards —
# never commit `.dev.vars` itself. For real deployments these are set with
# `wrangler secret put <NAME> --env <dev|production>`, never here and never
# in wrangler.jsonc (Task 0 secrets hygiene).
#
# /api/health (T1) needs no secrets. Listed now so the full secret surface
# is documented up front; each is wired by the task named beside it.

# T3 — Clerk JWT verification middleware. CLERK_JWT_KEY is read
# (@clerk/backend's `verifyToken()` -- NOT `verify()`; T3 found the plan's
# note about a consolidated `verify()` doesn't match what's installed,
# see the phase amendment). CLERK_SECRET_KEY is declared for parity/future
# use (e.g. T13's Clerk Admin API calls) but not read by T3's own code path.
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=
CLERK_JWT_KEY=

# T12 — email re-engagement (Resend + unsubscribe tokens)
RESEND_API_KEY=
UNSUB_HMAC_SECRET=

# v6 Phase 6.2a — Stripe backend (test mode only). STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is a
# `sk_test_...` key from the Stripe dashboard's test-mode API keys page --
# stripeClient.ts asserts its prefix matches wrangler.jsonc's STRIPE_MODE
# (F49), so a live key pasted here would fail loudly, not silently.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET is the `whsec_...` secret for the specific webhook
# endpoint you're testing against -- the Stripe CLI's `stripe listen` prints
# its own, DIFFERENT secret; don't cross-wire the two (Stripe's own
# signature-verification troubleshooting doc calls this out explicitly).
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
